`P(A or B) =` $answerbox[0]</p>

<p>Part (b): If you were to simply add the two individual probabilities (`$pA_disp + $pB_disp`), the resulting sum would be greater than the correct answer found in part (a). Why does this happen?
$answerbox[1]</p>

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION:  ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$categories = array("Red", "Blue", "Green", "Yellow");
$catNames = array("Red", "Blue", "Green", "Yellow");

// Generate counts that sum to a "nice" total for percentage calculations
// Total will be 50, 100, or 200 to ensure terminating decimals
$totals = array(50, 100, 200);
$total = randfrom($totals);

// Distribute the total across 4 categories
$counts = array();
$sum = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < 3; $i++) {
    // Ensure each category has at least 5 and doesn't exceed 70% of total
    $val = rand(5, floor($total * 0.6));
    $counts[$i] = $val;
    $sum += $val;
}
// The last category takes the remainder. 
$counts[3] = $total - $sum;

// If remainder is too small, we regenerate the set to ensure variety
if ($counts[3] < 5) {
    $total = randfrom($totals);
    $counts = array();
    $sum = 0;
    for ($i = 0; $i < 3; $i++) {
        $val = rand(5, floor($total * 0.6));
        $counts[$i] = $val;
        $sum += $val;
    }
    $counts[3] = $total - $sum;
}

// Calculate percentages
$percents = array();
for ($i = 0; $i < 4; $i++) {
    $percents[$i] = ($counts[$i] / $total) * 100;
}

// Pick one category for the calculation part
$targetIdx = rand(0, 3);
$targetCat = $catNames[$targetIdx];
$targetCount = $counts[$targetIdx];
$targetPerc = $percents[$targetIdx];

$answer[0] = $total;
$answer[1] = $targetPerc;
$answer[2] = 0; // Index for the choices part

$questions[2] = array(
    "The heights of the bars are scaled by the same constant, so the relative proportions remain the same.",
    "The percentages are calculated by adding a constant to each count, which preserves the shape.",
    "The total sample size is removed from the data, which flattens the distribution.",
    "The percentages are calculated by multiplying each count by the total, which changes the scale but not the shape."
);

$anstypes = "number,number,choices";

// Formatting for the tables
$tableCounts = "<table border='1' style='border-collapse: collapse; text-align: center;'><tr><th>Category</th><th>Count</th></tr>";
for ($i = 0; $i < 4; $i++) {
    $tableCounts .= "<tr><td>{$catNames[$i]}</td><td>{$counts[$i]}</td></tr>";
}
$tableCounts .= "</table>";

$tablePercs = "<table border='1' style='border-collapse: collapse; text-align: center;'><tr><th>Category</th><th>Percentage</th></tr>";
for ($i = 0; $i < 4; $i++) {
    $tablePercs .= "<tr><td>{$catNames[$i]}</td><td>" . round($percents[$i], 1) . "%</td></tr>";
}
$tablePercs .= "</table>";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A researcher collected data on the favorite colors of a group of participants. The data is summarized in the two tables below.</p>

<p><strong>Table 1: Counts</strong><br/>$tableCounts</p>
<p><strong>Table 2: Percentages</strong><br/>$tablePercs</p>

<p>Part (a): Based on the tables provided, what was the total number of participants in the study?</p>
<p>Total participants: $answerbox[0]</p>

<p>Part (b): What is the exact percentage of participants who chose {$targetCat}?</p>
<p>Percentage for {$targetCat}: $answerbox[1]%</p>

<p>Part (c): If the researcher creates a bar graph of the counts and a bar graph of the percentages, the two graphs will have the exact same shape (the bars will be in the same relative proportions). Why is this the case?</p>
<p>$answerbox[2]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$sumString = arraytolist($counts, ' + ');
$solutionguide = 'Part (a): The total number of participants is the sum of all counts in Table 1: ' . $sumString . ' = ' . $total . '. <br/>Part (b): The percentage for ' . $targetCat . ' is calculated as (Count of ' . $targetCat . ' / Total) * 100, which is (' . $targetCount . ' / ' . $total . ') * 100 = ' . $targetPerc . '%. <br/>Part (c): To convert counts to percentages, every count is divided by the same total (the sample size). Since every bar is scaled by the same constant factor (1/total), the relative heights of the bars do not change, preserving the shape of the graph.';

